"The last lesson matched each of the seven emotions to an organ it can injure, but Chinese medicine goes one step further, describing exactly how an emotion causes damage: through its effect on the movement of qi. Anger is said to make qi rise — which is why prolonged anger is associated with headaches, a flushed face, and a pounding sensation, all symptoms of qi surging upward. Fear is said to make qi descend and scatter — which is why sudden, intense fear is associated with a sinking feeling in the stomach or a loss of bladder control.": "9a1425d7e3d3",
"The last lesson matched each of the seven emotions to an organ it can injure, but Chinese medicine goes one step further, describing exactly how an emotion causes damage: through its effect on the movement of qi. Anger is said to make qi rise — which is why prolonged anger is associated with headaches, a flushed face, and a pounding sensation, all symptoms of qi surging upward. Fear is said to make qi descend and scatter — which is why sudden, intense fear is associated with a sinking feeling in the stomach or a loss of bladder control. Worry and overthinking are said to \"knot\" qi, causing it to stagnate in one place rather than flow — which shows up as a tight sensation in the chest or stomach, and a struggle to make decisions or move forward. Grief is said to drain qi directly, matching the exhaustion and shortness of breath that so often follow real loss. Joy, in its ordinary form, is said to relax and smooth qi's movement, which is part of why Chinese medicine considers joy protective rather than harmful in typical amounts — it is only sudden, overstimulating joy that is treated as potentially disruptive. This mechanism explains why the emotional causes of illness in Chinese medicine were never meant to be treated as separate from the physical ones. An emotion isn't just a feeling that happens to accompany a symptom — in this framework, it is a specific, describable disturbance in how qi is moving, with its own physical signature. This is also why simply telling someone to \"calm down\" does very little: the goal is restoring qi's normal movement, not suppressing the emotion itself.": "cd99b66ffd05",
